welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Reema Salah an assistant clinical  professor at the Yale Child Study Center  and former deputy executive director of  UNICEF today we'll talk with Professor  Saleh about her recently published book  pathways to peace the transformative  power of women and children that she  worked on in collaboration with dr.  James Lachman and Catherine Panzer brick  from Yale University  welcome professor Saleh thank you very  much it's a great pleasure to be with  you thank you so afternoon  let's begin with an overview of the book  tell us about it okay the book is  pathways to peace the transformative  power of families and children and it  all started in 2013 when Ernest Rickman  which is a forum in Germany convened a  meeting to review a very important  premise do the way we're a the ways we  raise our children have an impact on  peace or help to promote peace also  another question can peaceful childhood  create a culture of peace and as you  know there is more and more evidence  that the how we invest in the early  years of the child can have an impact in  creating a culture of peace and we  thought by presenting this book we can  put our hands with the international  community and contribute to the debate  on peace building creating a culture of  peace and particularly justice and peace  in society it also started with the  journey of mr. Lachman and professor  Lachman who went to Turkey and met with  an organization a non-governmental  organization mother-child foundation  and they had programs in early childhood  development development in all the  country in Turkey in villages in cities  in and within communities and they so  they realized that early childhood  development the programs had an impact  in creating peace at home in the  community and at large so they asked  professor Blackman they wanted the  scientific evidence and it all started  from there also it started with the  launch of a consortium on early  childhood development that we launched  at UNICEF in 2012 and also to join the  forces of the international community to  find answers and ways to really build a  peaceful society but not only peaceful  inclusive and that is why it is  important so we thought we will bring  you know the evidence of knowledge the  evidence and inform that debate and it  was very timely why is it timely because  of the situation in the world millions  and millions of children are living in  environments of violence and in the book  it categorizes violence in two ways we  have the direct violence like when  children have you know abuse and all the  war the direct impact of war but also  there is the structural violence which  is very important which is the poverty  which is marginalization which is  oppression and also rights and  children's rights that are not conformed  to the Convention on the Rights of the  Child so we thought that we that our  contribution will help millions and  millions of children okay now there are  a number of contributors to the book so  you've you've collected research from a  number of people can you tell us who are  some of the contributors and what were  some of their findings we had more than  40 contributors and they came from all  Sciences so it is multi  disciplinary and that is the strength  and the power of it and so we had for  example anthropologists sociologists  biologists neurobiologist and also  people from peacebuilding and also from  religious studies so it was so important  that they had to answer questions for  example does human biological  development impact peace and this is  something new this is groundbreaking  science that is very important and does  it yes does it does because the some of  the findings say that when children are  exposed to violence is detrimental to  their development they will not develop  to their full potential and also it  affects the brain how important the  brain development in the early years of  life and then all their experience in  the first year even of life will  determine the capacity of the brains for  life and also how important is to build  a good brain you have to have good care  child caring you have to have also in  like involve parents in so that they in  they put cognitive and emotional and all  these aspects of them are very important  but also health and nutrition so it is a  holistic approach but the most important  thing is safety and protection how to  create loving environment how to create  environments that care for children and  particularly in times of war and  conflict or where children are refugees  and n displaced and also how important  is for example bonding and this is very  important and the role of parents and  good parenting because as I said this  will you know have an great impact on  the brain and can be transmitted across  generations and this is what we say the  study of epigenetics which is also  groundbreaking  so and so in this we know that good  early childhood care positive parenting  has a great impact on children and  particularly under future life and this  was through research that was done by  the contributors yes by the contributors  and we had the other research for  example other contribution to the book  what are the good interventions what are  so they showcased interventions from  United States to Nigeria to South Africa  to Lebanon to Turkey that show how can  good interventions also create an  environment of peace  can you speak to some of the specific  interventions that were done yes sure  for example I spoke about a little bit  the interventions in Turkey but also  interventions for example in Lebanon  where we work with mothers in  Palestinian refugee camps and as you see  as you know the environment in a camp  are not always positive so working with  mothers had a great impact on the  relationship with their children and not  only with the relationship with the  children but also the centre's had an  impact in creating cohesion in the  community okay so when you say it's  important from you know mothers to work  with their children or what specifically  was being done with the women in the  camps to foster that yeah for example  they followed a few weeks of training  and talking and and working with them  how to deal with the child how when  their child is violent what should they  do do they spank their children or not  sometimes you spank children so they  learn that this is not the the best way  how to listen to their children so all  this has really a great impact as I said  in the family but also in a community  that is not very positive right and also  in South Africa for example because  this will help when they mothers meet  when parents meet the role of the father  is very important when they meet in the  centers they transcend the divide of  religious divide ethnic divide because  they become mothers together and fathers  together to take care of their children  right so the last chapter of the book  talks about effective policies and  programs that can alleviate violence and  promote peace  can you talk in specifics about some of  them yes that the the most important  thing is how to transmit the science  because if we want science and keep it  in the libraries if not it is not  important it's how you transmit it how  can communities how can government's how  can the United Nations  you know benefit from this so we have to  transmit it so if so that we have  policies so that governments also for  example realize that early childhood  development are important and they  should be owned by the countries should  be owned by the by the government they  should put budgets for you know for them  because sometimes they think especially  if countries are poorer they will try to  put budgets for other things but not for  early childhood development but I think  sharing this information and in this  chapter we have recommendations at all  levels recommendation for governments  for example we have recommendations for  the United Nations and so member states  of the United Nations who are working on  peace and security have to know that  early childhood development should be  part of all the peace building programs  and on the community level it's very  important that for example mayors and  local governments for example we have  examples of mayors for children or  mayor's for the rights of children in  Italy in Japan and in other places how  can we have mayors for peace how can  we have mayor's for early childhood  development so what we are doing and  what is the most important goal for us  is to have a resolution at the United  Nations a resolution that states that  families and children are important  agents of change and important agents of  peace so the resolution we are working  on it to be adopted next year then it  can because a resolution at the United  Nations can mobilize all member states  193 member states but also the role of  nongovernmental organizations is very  important because sometimes when non  governments cannot do cannot implement  everything you know civil society is  important to help and we have examples  of programs in many countries that are  led by nongovernmental organizations but  most importantly how to share this  knowledge and the translator of this  knowledge is very important how to give  the router guide communities how to  guide governments it's very important  and also very important in respecting  cultures I was going to ask you altars  must play a large role here from country  to country and it's I think they're  important to recognize that in terms of  trying to implement yes for instance can  you have you any experience with can you  cite any specific examples of how  culture will come into play in any  particular for example even if I go back  to the culture in Lebanon I mean the  culture is very important because you  cannot tell mothers that all what they  were doing is wrong so you have to bring  it in a way to guide them in the new way  in the and to translate this  groundbreaking science to them in a  language that they understand and that  is not against for example their culture  and their and their traditions of course  there are sometimes harmful traditional  practices that little by little stir  by step as also with my work in UNICEF  we did so step by step you can guide  them to get rid of those traditions for  example of spanking a child or for all  these traditions so it's important and  we have a whole chapter that talks about  culture and also we have a chapter where  you have also involvement as I said from  religious inter-religious dialogue  because in a community you have  everybody and you have to involve  everybody you're having to involve the  leaders the local governments you have  traditional Chiefs religious leaders  civil society itself and mother fathers  and the older caregivers the teachers  for example the pediatricians in there  in that community so how to join forces  together to bring peace through the  transformative power of early childhood  development right so if there is one  thing that you would like readers to  take from the book what would it be that  families and children are not only  victims in times of war and conflicts  are not only victims to violence but are  agents of change through the  transformative power of early childhood  development thank you so much for being  here with us thank your worth thank you  it's a great pleasure and just to say  that it was a great pleasure and honor  to work with the two other professors  professor Lachman James Lachman and  Catherine Panther Brick who are also  known for their science but for example  professor panther break for her work on  resilience and we have a lot in our book  on really resilience how to build  resilience of communities and and  families especially in times of conflict  and of course all the work of professor  Lichtman on childhood very good thank  you so much thank you thank you for more  information about Professor Salah and  her research please visit our website at  macmillan report yale.edu be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  McMillon Center for International and  area studies at Yale
 

